{"author":"Rebar Bot Nava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Implementation notes after plan review. (1) Worst-case wait: write_lock defaults (timeout 30, attempts 2) give a 60 s window per pass, and write_path_retries() (default 2) gives 3 passes, so about 180 s plus jittered backoff, the same budget every canonical store write already takes. (2) _merge_ref has 3 callers: the candidate merge (a lock failure fails the close, the case reproduced here) and the 2 post-publication convergence paths (_converge_fetched_publication and the already-present path), which on a lock failure record pending local sync and return a *_local_pending result; they now wait the same canonical budget before doing so, which is intended: the remote already holds the close, and a longer wait only makes convergence more likely to finish in-process. (3) Added test_candidate_merge_waits_on_the_canonical_write_lock_budget (patches write_lock, asserts dual_window=True and retries=write_path_retries() with no fixed timeout), so a regression to timeout=10, attempts=1 fails CI. Module: 19 passed. A 4 s-per-start shim run of the race test with the fix is in progress."},"env_id":"492a5c71-6cda-48a6-8134-02ff6a672739","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1795245730823774001,"uuid":"14b4db16-62cf-44ab-9914-88ad8c99a4b3"}